## Configure and command

Every script keeps its settings in the Programmable Block's **Custom Data** (ini; written on first run, edit and run `reload`).
Commands go in the PB's *argument* (run the PB with the text, or put it on a toolbar slot / button panel) or arrive over IGC on the channel `<IGC Tag>_CMD`.
Printed drones start in IDLE and wait for the printer's `_JOIN`; a drone pasted by hand needs `guard` or `hunt` (or set `Start Mode`).

- `guard` holds the spot where the drone was pasted (or where you last ran `home`) and attacks anything within `Guard Radius` (1.5 km) of it.
  With nothing in sight it stays put and turns in place to scan; it does not patrol.
- `hunt` attacks anything it finds. After 15 s with nothing found it flies a search square of 1.2 km around home, stopping every leg to look
  around; several hunters fan out in different directions.

## AI blocks (Full variants): switch AI Behavior on

The Full drones carry an AI Offensive and an AI Flight block (named `... [AI]`). They are the drones' long-range eyes: they notice enemies up
to 2.5 km away in every direction, the script confirms with its cameras and takes over the fight. **A blueprint cannot pre-set them: they
paste with AI Behavior off and do nothing.** The scripts try to switch AI Behavior on by themselves; the PB screen shows
`AI Behavior on (2 blocks)` if that worked, or `AI Behavior switch not found: turn AI Behavior on by hand`.

To set them up by hand (once, then save the drone with Ctrl+B as your own blueprint so every copy keeps it):
1. `<Name> AI Flight [AI]`: Speed Limit 60-100 m/s, Collision Avoidance on, AI Behavior **on**.
2. `<Name> AI Offensive [AI]`: attack Enemies, Target Priority Closest, Facing Mode "Use Static Weapons" with the drone's guns moved to
   Selected, AI Behavior **on**.
3. Leave both blocks switched **off** (Enabled): the script switches them on and off itself.

The status line `AI: searching` means the AI blocks have the ship, `AI: own search` means the script is sweeping its cameras. Without
working AI blocks (and on Lite variants) a drone searches with camera rays only: it reliably finds enemies within about 100 m, and farther
ones slowly, mostly once they shoot at it.

